        "Christian Pfleiderer",
        "Yoshinori Tokura"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Magnetic skyrmions revealed a robust topological form of magnetic order and emergent electrodynamics in real materials, opening a substantial new area of magnetism.",
        "attribution": "Pfleiderer and Tokura are leading experimental discoverers and builders of the field; the broader theoretical and experimental record should inform final attribution.",
        "maturity": "Skyrmions have been observed and studied across multiple materials and experimental settings, though proposed applications remain under development.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The route from discovery to a single, sharply defined prize contribution is broad.",
          "The practical significance of skyrmion-based devices is not yet settled."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 4,
      "ballot_id": "B010",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Claudio Castelnovo",
        "Roderich Moessner",
        "Steven T. Bramwell"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "Spin ice provides an unusually clear material realization of fractionalized magnetic excitations and a Coulomb phase, linking elegant theory to measurable phenomena.",
        "attribution": "Castelnovo and Moessner developed the influential monopole interpretation, and Bramwell contributed importantly to its experimental establishment.",
        "maturity": "The central spin-ice phenomenology is mature and supported by extensive measurements.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The monopoles are emergent quasiparticles in a material, so the distinction between the striking interpretation and the underlying observations matters for the citation."
        ]
      }
    },
    {
      "rank": 5,
      "ballot_id": "B015",
      "proposed_laureates": [
        "Michael Berry",
        "Yakir Aharonov"
      ],
      "assessment": {
        "nobel_worthiness": "The Aharonov-Bohm effect and Berry phase exposed the physical role of quantum phases and geometric structure, with influence extending across quantum physics and condensed matter.",
        "attribution": "Aharonov's effect and Berry's general phase are distinct contributions; the proposed pair captures both, though their histories and wider antecedents need careful treatment.",
        "maturity": "Both ideas have been confirmed and applied for decades across many experimental and theoretical settings.",
        "uncertainties": [
          "The discovery is a broad conceptual family rather than a single bounded result, complicating scope and citation."
        ]
      }
    },
